import lsp_types::Position

-- | A protocol position as a byte offset into `source` — plan 71 leaf 5.
-- |
-- | [`position_of`]'s inverse, and the direction every *cursor* capability
-- | needs: the editor sends a line and a UTF-16 character, and every span in
-- | the AST is a pair of byte offsets.
-- |
-- | # UTF-16 in, bytes out
-- |
-- | `position_of` sums `char::len_utf16` to produce the character column, so
-- | this undoes exactly that sum rather than counting `char`s. A line holding
-- | an astral character — an emoji, a rare CJK ideograph — has a UTF-16 column
-- | two greater than its `char` column at every position after it, and
-- | `position_of`'s own doc comment is the reason the protocol wants it that
-- | way. Counting `char`s here would put the cursor in the wrong place in
-- | exactly the files Redox's own source contains, which carry `—` and `§`
-- | throughout.
-- |
-- | # Out of range clamps rather than failing
-- |
-- | A position past the end of a line, or past the end of the file, answers
-- | with the nearest real offset. The editor and the server see the same
-- | document under full sync (plan 40 decision 5), so a position out of range
-- | is a race rather than a mistake — the user typed while the request was in
-- | flight — and the kind answer is the nearest offset rather than an error
-- | reply in their log.
export(crate) offset_of : &str -> Position -> usize
offset_of source at =
  wanted_line = at.line : u32
  -- The byte offset each line starts at, in order. `split_inclusive` keeps the
  -- `\n` with its line, so the running total is the next line's start.
  mut offset = 0 : usize
  -- `enumerate` rather than a counter, which clippy's `explicit_counter_loop`
  -- asks for by name. The index is a `usize`, so the comparison casts rather
  -- than the counter being typed — one conversion at the test instead of an
  -- increment per line.
  for pair in .enumerate (.split_inclusive '\n' source)
    line : u32 = .unwrap_or u32::MAX (.ok (u32::try_from pair.0))
    text = pair.1
    if line == wanted_line
      -- Walk the line's characters, accumulating UTF-16 units until the
      -- requested column is reached. The `\n` is included in `text`, which is
      -- harmless: a column past the line's content clamps at its end.
      mut units = 0 : u32
      mut within = 0 : usize
      for ch in .chars text
        if units >= at.character
          break
        if ch == '\n'
          break
        units += .unwrap_or u32::MAX (.ok (u32::try_from (char::len_utf16 ch)))
        within += .len_utf8 ch
      return offset + within
    offset += .len text
  -- The requested line is past the end of the document.
  .len source
